.TH SYNCTANG\-NETWORK.CONF 5 "" "synctang" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
synctang\-network.conf \- choose the relay and discovery servers for synctang
.SH DESCRIPTION
By default synctang uses the public Syncthing relay pool and discovery
servers.
This file, and the options that override it, name your own instead.
Everything in it is optional, and an absent file means the public servers.
.PP
The machine listens: it joins one relay and announces that relay's address,
under its Device ID, to a discovery server.
A key holder dials: it looks the machine up on a discovery server, is handed the
relay address, and joins the same relay.
So both sides must agree on the discovery server, and only the machine needs a
relay setting.
.PP
A wrong value is an error that stops the command and names the setting.
It never falls back to the public servers: that would announce, on the servers
the owner chose not to use, a machine the owner chose not to announce there.
Error messages name the setting and never repeat the URL, which may carry
something private.
.SH FORMAT
One
.IB key = value
per line.
Blank lines and lines starting with
.B #
are ignored, and space around the key and the value is removed.
There is no quoting.
An empty value means the setting is not set.
An unknown key, a line without
.BR = ,
or a key given twice is an error that names the line.
.SH KEYS
.TP
\fBrelay\-pool\fR
Where the machine learns relays from.
Either the URL of a relay pool,
.B http://
or
.BR https:// ,
or
.BI relay:// host : port /?id= relay-device-id
for one relay run by the owner, which is then the only relay used.
The relay's Device ID is printed by
.B strelaysrv
at start-up in its
.B URI:
line.
Only the machine has this setting; a key holder's file refuses it.
.TP
\fBdiscovery\fR
The discovery server, used to look a machine up and, unless
.B discovery\-announce
is set, to announce to.
See
.BR "DISCOVERY URLS" .
.TP
\fBdiscovery\-announce\fR
Where announcements are posted, if that differs from
.BR discovery .
Always
.BR https .
.SH DISCOVERY URLS
A discovery server usually has a self-signed certificate.
The URL says how to trust it, in the same syntax Syncthing uses:
.TP
\fB?id=\fR\fIDevice ID\fR
The server's certificate must be exactly this Device ID, however it is signed.
No certificate authority is involved.
It is checked in the TLS handshake on every connection, before any request is
sent; a different certificate is refused with an error naming both Device IDs.
.TP
\fB?insecure\fR
Do not verify the server's certificate at all.
.B ?insecure=false
means verify.
.PP
With neither, the system's certificate authorities decide, and a self-signed
server is refused.
Both options are taken out of the URL before anything is sent to the server;
any other query is kept.
An
.B id
must be a well-formed Device ID.
.PP
Plain
.B http://
is for looking up only, never for announcing.
An announcement says where the machine is, and is never sent over plain http.
So the
.B discovery
setting of a machine, which it announces to, must be
.BR https ,
unless
.B discovery\-announce
names an
.B https
endpoint of its own.
A key holder's
.B discovery
may be
.B http://
only with
.BR ?insecure ,
and
.B ?id=
is not accepted with
.BR http:// ,
since there is no certificate to pin.
.B discovery\-announce
is always
.BR https .
A machine's
.B ?id=
also pins the announcement: it is posted only to the server with that Device ID.
.PP
When the machine's discovery server is set,
.B synctang\-unlocker pair
puts it in the pairing link, so that the phone looks the machine up there.
The relay and the announce endpoint are not put in the link.
.PP
Example:
.PP
.nf
discovery=https://192.0.2.20:8443/v2/?id=<server Device ID>
.fi
.PP
One thing to know when testing:
.BR stdiscosrv ,
the Syncthing project's discovery server, drops loopback addresses, so a relay
reachable only as
.B 127.0.0.1
is never found: use its LAN address.
.SH PRECEDENCE
From weakest to strongest: the file, then the kernel arguments, then flags.
.PP
On the machine, the file is read by
.BR "synctang\-unlocker agent" ,
.B pair
and
.BR open ,
and the kernel arguments
.B rd.synctang.relay=
and
.B rd.synctang.discovery=
override it.
A discovery server given on the kernel command line replaces the file's
.B discovery
and its
.B discovery\-announce
together, since the announce endpoint belonged to the server it replaces.
.PP
On a key holder, the file is read by
.BR "synctang\-keyholder unlock" ,
and
.B \-\-discovery
and
.B \-\-discovery\-announce
override it, in the same way.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/synctang/network.conf
The machine's file.
Named by
.B \-\-network\-conf
of
.BR synctang\-unlocker .
The dracut module copies it into the initrd when it exists, so rebuild the
initrd (for example with
.BR "dracut \-\-force" )
after changing it.
.TP
.I <user\ config\ dir>/synctang\-keyholder/config
A key holder's file, with the keys
.B discovery
and
.B discovery\-announce
only.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR synctang\-unlocker (8),
.BR synctang\-keyholder (1)
